*To the Reader*

The goal of this book is not to be as easy to read as possible.

Readability is an important objective for educational materials, but it is often pursued at the expense of precision. When concepts are oversimplified or definitions are replaced with everyday descriptions, the resulting understanding remains superficial. Furthermore, if a textbook relies heavily on informal language and storytelling, students may find it difficult to distinguish what is essential from what merely serves as illustration.

This book is not intended to be read independently of classroom instruction. Although it is not meant to replace teaching, the text has been written as precisely and consistently as possible so that students can revisit the material and, when necessary, review and consolidate the key concepts on their own.

In the early stages of learning physics, concepts are acquired through observation, measurement, and experimentation. During lessons, students investigate physical phenomena through experiments. The purpose of the concepts introduced in this book is then to give those observations and methods precise meaning.

Once a solid foundation has been established for investigating phenomena and understanding the fundamental concepts, students can move on to the mathematical tools used to describe, explain, and predict those phenomena. This book does not shy away from mathematical descriptions either. A direct proportionality is introduced as a mathematical relationship between two physical quantities, enabling students to understand where the equations and mathematical models used in physics come from.

Many of the concepts introduced in this book—such as model, theory, experimentation, and graph and direct proportionality—will reappear throughout the study of physics. Understanding these concepts helps students recognize that physics is not merely a collection of formulas or explanations of individual phenomena, but a way of investigating and understanding the natural world.

I hope this book not only teaches the content of physics, but also demonstrates how physicists—and scientists more generally—think, what kinds of research methods they use, and how scientific knowledge is presented.

The principles introduced here extend far beyond physics. They form the foundation of modern science and shape the way knowledge is produced, evaluated, and applied in today's world. In doing so, they have also profoundly influenced the modern understanding of the world.

